id: k8s.top-resource-usage
kind: kubernetes
title: Resource usage (kubectl top)
aliases: kubectl top, resource usage
tags: operations, resources, performance
questions:
  - how do you see actual CPU and memory usage?
invariant: `kubectl top` reports live usage from the metrics pipeline, which is separate from the scheduler's view of requests.
answer_30s: `kubectl top pod|node` shows measured usage, which you compare against declared requests and limits to right-size things.
answer_2m: |
  Two mental models to keep apart: the scheduler plans with requests, the
  autoscaler may scale on usage, and the kernel enforces limits. `top` shows the
  third-party metrics pipeline (metrics-server) output, so it needs that
  component installed -- and it is instantaneous, not a history. For capacity
  and right-sizing questions the honest answer is a time series (Prometheus,
  container_memory_working_set_bytes, CPU throttling counters), because a
  point-in-time sample cannot distinguish a leak from a burst. Usage well below
  requests wastes cluster money; usage above limits produces OOMKills and
  throttling.
when_to_use:
  - right-sizing requests/limits, spotting a leak or an OOMKill candidate
commands:
  - kubectl top pod -A => current usage per Pod across the cluster
  - kubectl top node => node usage vs allocatable capacity
  - kubectl top pod --containers => per-container breakdown in a multi-container Pod
failure_modes:
  - metrics-server missing, so top returns "error: Metrics API not available"
  - treating one snapshot as capacity planning data
related:
  - k8s.resources.requests
  - k8s.resources.limits
  - design.observability
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_top/
